P='src-b89-'
rows=[
 ('descender','Descender (comics)','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Descender_(comics)','secondary_plot_reference','Harvester attacks trigger robot culling; actual mining and companion robots exist.','Neither the pre-cull Council economy nor later robot survival supplies ordinary human material access rules.'),
 ('harvesters','Descender 27','Image Comics','https://imagecomics.com/comics/releases/descender-27','publisher_issue_description','The issue reveals ancient robot and Harvester origins.','Origins and planet-sized robots do not establish a human-derived provision population.'),
 ('alex','Alex + Ada','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alex_%2B_Ada','secondary_plot_reference','Restricted android consciousness and later legal rights shape the relationship.','Companionship, rights and body ownership alone are not an automatic human economy.'),
 ('notall','Not All Robots','AWA Studios','https://awastudios.com/series/not-all-robots/','publisher_description','Robots replace the human workforce; each human family receives a robot on which it depends.','Assignment is not proof of secure enforceable welfare or equal human control.'),
 ('notall-review','Not All Robots volume 1 review','The Comics Beat reviewer','https://www.comicsbeat.com/trade-rating-not-all-robots-vol-1-the-award-winning-comic-lives-up-to-the-hype/','secondary_review','Robot-run bubble cities, surviving human hairdressers and catastrophic oxygen failure expose dependency.','The cause of the air failure is disputed; purposeful sabotage is not asserted as fact.'),
 ('magnus','Magnus, Robot Fighter','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magnus,_Robot_Fighter','secondary_setting_reference','Robot-dependent North Am, class divisions and a later benevolent Freewill Steel Nation are distinct.','Human dependence and robot freedom do not establish universal ordinary allocation; separate reboots are not merged.'),
 ('dredd','Mega-City One','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mega-City_One','secondary_lore_reference','Robotics displaces employment; welfare, inadequate housing and concentrated Judge power coexist.','Historical unemployment figures and city geography vary; no figure is silently copied into all-person nonworking.'),
 ('dredd-rules','Judge Dredd Mongoose core entertainment passage','Mongoose Publishing rule text indexed in a public mirror','https://files.spawningpool.net/docs/Vault2.0.-.TTRPG-Gamebooks/Judge%20Dredd/Judge%20Dredd%20Mongoose%20RPG%20-%20Core%20Rules.pdf','indexed_rule_passage','Robots do hard, monotonous and skilled jobs; citizens have abundant leisure and a stated unemployment figure.','Bounded indexed section only; no full PDF retrieval, current-rule assertion or new movie economy claim.'),
 ('mechanismo','Mechanismo','Judge Dredd Wiki contributors','https://judgedredd.fandom.com/wiki/Mechanismo','secondary_lore_reference','Robot Judges supplement depleted human Justice Department forces after disasters.','Police deployment does not itself transfer all productive control or constitute a separate civilian economy.'),
 ('verdus','Robo-Hunter','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robo-Hunter','secondary_plot_reference','Verdus robots prepare a colony but imprison arriving humans as simulated impostors.','Confinement after terraforming is not a verified work-independent civilian floor.'),
 ('abc','ABC Warriors','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Warriors','secondary_plot_reference','War robots and later conflict on Mars coexist with farms and human labor.','Military robot autonomy is not a human necessities system.'),
 ('metalzoic','Metalzoic','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metalzoic','secondary_plot_reference','After humanity abandons Earth, evolved robotic species inhabit a competitive machine ecology.','An ecosystem of artificial persons is not automatically human leisure; no active human-derived scope is established.'),
 ('halo','The Ballad of Halo Jones','Wikipedia contributors','https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Ballad_of_Halo_Jones','secondary_plot_reference','The Hoop houses unemployed increased-leisure citizens; Halo later takes useful steward work.','Housing segregation and unemployment do not establish their technological cause or a durable automated floor.')
]
S=cited_sources(P,rows)
refs=[P+'notall',P+'notall-review']
R=[scoped_record('c0764','not-all-robots-bubble-families','Not All Robots — Robot-supported bubble families','Not All Robots','Human households dependent on assigned robot providers',['comic'],
 'Original AWA comic, opening social arrangement','2056 bubble-city life before the catastrophic air-system failure','Living humans in functioning robot-supported bubble households',
 ['Robots as humans in the participation denominator','Oxygen-death victims as nonworking survivors','Intentional sabotage as a settled fact'],
 'In 2056, robots have replaced almost the entire human workforce and each family is assigned a robot provider. People live in enclosed cities whose essential systems are also managed by robots. This is implemented dependence, not merely a forecast of future automation. Guarantees receive only a weak favorable estimate because household assignment supplies ongoing support while leaving families exposed to their providers and fragile city life-support systems. The later oxygen catastrophe demonstrates how consequential that dependence can be.\n\nRobot control of work and the cities places effective authority near the autonomous-machine end. That does not make every robot benevolent or every individual provider all-powerful. A high, broad nonworking estimate follows the explicit replacement of human work and the exception of hairdressers, allowing for useful care, artistic activity, organization and other unpaid contributions. It is not a literal claim that all humans are idle. The point covers living residents before the air-system disaster; dead residents are not counted as liberated workers, and the review’s uncertainty over sabotage remains unresolved.',
 [-.3,-.95,.85],[[-.75,.65],[-1,-.65],[.5,1]],
 ['The publisher explicitly establishes assigned robot support for every family; city life-support dependency makes its security uncertain.','Robots control the main workforce and bubble-city infrastructure; human dependence does not establish effective human ownership.','Near-total replacement with hairdressers as an explicit exception supports .85 [.5,1], a noncanonical broad estimate allowing useful unpaid work.'],refs,5)]
refs=[P+'dredd',P+'dredd-rules']
R.append(scoped_record('c0767','judge-dredd-mega-city-one','Judge Dredd — Mega-City One’s robot economy','Judge Dredd','Robot production and welfare under the Justice Department',['comic','tabletop'],
 'Comic city background and its Mongoose tabletop adaptation','Established police-state robot economy, outside a particular citywide catastrophe','Living human residents of Mega-City One',
 ['A particular movie’s economy without separate verification','Every historical unemployment figure as one contemporaneous census','Destroyed or dead citizens as leisure beneficiaries'],
 'Robots perform most ordinary production and services in Mega-City One, including skilled tasks as well as drudgery. Human employment becomes scarce and welfare supports a large dependent population. Material guarantees are rated weak rather than absent: benefits exist, but inadequate housing and official hostility to recipients make a secure universal floor doubtful. The estimate concerns the established system, not a moment when a catastrophe has destroyed ordinary provision.\n\nThe Justice Department concentrates political authority and governs the robot-supported city. Machines doing jobs do not automatically become the sovereign, so control remains near the concentrated human-elite position. The setting describes widespread boredom and abundant leisure, supporting a high but broad editorial nonworking estimate. Published unemployment numbers vary by period and measure formal employment, not all useful activity; none is copied directly as this axis. Care, art, study and informal work can continue. The verified comic and tabletop depictions are tagged, while film adaptations are left out until their economic arrangements are separately established.',
 [.5,.05,.8],[[0,.9],[0,.2],[.45,.98]],
 ['Automatic production and substantial welfare coexist with inadequate housing and explicit official hostility toward welfare recipients.','The human Justice Department retains concentrated authority; productive robots are not by themselves rulers.','Extensive leisure and displaced work support .8 [.45,.98] as an editorial direction; varying formal-unemployment figures are not a census of useful activity.'],refs,3,alternatives=[1]))

cases=[
 ('c0761','needs_evidence','descender','The UGC robot premise does not establish ordinary human allocation before or after the cull.','Mining and companion roles are real robot work, but robot numbers do not establish a labor-independent household floor.'),
 ('c0762','boundary_case','harvesters','Harvester origins do not establish a human-derived provision society.','Planet-sized artificial entities and destructive intervention are not an applicable leisure population.'),
 ('c0763','needs_evidence','alex','Android consciousness restrictions do not establish human work-independent provision.','The romance and later android rights answer personhood questions, leaving ordinary production and access unresolved.'),
 ('c0765','needs_evidence','magnus','North Am’s robot dependence lacks a sufficiently clear necessities-allocation mechanism in the short reference.','Complacent upper classes, human elites and lower-level outcasts cannot be merged into universal welfare.'),
 ('c0766','needs_evidence','magnus','The Freewill Steel Nation lacks a justified human-derived population and material-access scope.','Benevolent independent robots are recognized persons, but their freedom alone is not post-work provision.'),
 ('c0769','boundary_case','verdus','Verdus imprisons incoming humans rather than demonstrating their ordinary post-work civic life.','Robot terraforming and a mistaken identity hierarchy do not establish durable human support independent of work.'),
 ('c0770','needs_evidence','abc','Warrior robots and Martian farm conflict do not establish ordinary automatic provision.','Useful military work and human farm labor remain visible; no civilian access rules were established.'),
 ('c0771','boundary_case','metalzoic','Post-human machine ecology has no established active human-derived beneficiary population.','Robotic tribal survival and evolved bodies are not human leisure.'),
 ('c0772','needs_evidence','halo','The Hoop’s unemployment ghetto lacks a verified automation-funded ordinary floor.','Increased-leisure is an in-universe euphemism; it must not be treated as proof that all useful work has disappeared.')
]
